In modern MSTest (v3.6+), you can use **constructor injection** for `TestContext` and implement `IAsyncInitialization` for async setup:

```csharp
using Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting;

[TestClass]
public class MyTests : IAsyncInitialization
{
    public TestContext TestContext { get; }

    // Constructor injection for TestContext + sync init
    public MyTests(TestContext testContext)
    {
        TestContext = testContext;
        // Synchronous initialization here
    }

    // Async initialization (runs after constructor)
    public Task InitializeAsync()
    {
        // Async setup work here
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public void MyTest()
    {
        Assert.IsNotNull(TestContext);
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- Constructor injection of `TestContext` replaces the old property-setter pattern (`public TestContext TestContext { get; set; }`)
- `IAsyncInitialization.InitializeAsync()` runs once after the constructor, before any `[TestInitialize]` methods
- You can still use `[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]` alongside this pattern for per-test setup
- This requires **MSTest 3.6+** (the `Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting` package)
